Subject: feat(fetch): allow a forge that is only reachable privately

The fetcher pre-resolves DNS and refuses any host resolving to a private or
reserved address. That is the right default, and it makes the CLI unusable
against an enterprise forge on an internal network: every fetch fails with
`resolved IP is internal/reserved`, including the agent harnesses a run needs,
even though the host is precisely the one the run is configured to use.

Add a host-scoped exception rather than a way to turn the check off.
FetchPolicy gains PrivateHosts, an exception list: a host named there skips
internal-IP validation, every other host is validated exactly as before, and a
host must also appear in AllowedDomains to be fetched at all, so two separate
lists have to name it. Matching is exact, with no wildcards, because a wildcard
exemption is how a narrow allowance becomes a broad one.

The default list is empty unless an operator sets FULLSEND_ALLOW_PRIVATE_FORGE=1,
and even then it holds at most one entry, the host GITHUB_SERVER_URL already
points at. The flag names no host itself, so it cannot be used to reach an
arbitrary internal service; it can only exempt the forge the run is configured
against. github.com and its subdomains are never exempt, since they have public
addresses and exempting them could only ever help an attacker who could make
them resolve privately.

This is deliberately not NewTestPolicy's skipIPCheck, which remains unexported
and unreachable from production code.

Breadboard note: patches Go source, so it belongs to the build patch list in
deploy/scripts/05i-build-fullsend.sh, and it applies on top of patch 0005.
Written to be sent upstream as-is. Add a DCO sign-off before submitting it as a
human-authored change.

